{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[agreed|Agreed]], [[contracted]].
#* {{RQ:Eddison Worm|page=20|passage=O Gorice XI., most glorious King of Witchland, and O Lord Goldry Bluszco, captain of the hosts of Demonland, it is '''compact''' betwixt you, and made fast by mighty oaths whereof I, the Red Foliot, am keeper, that ye shall wrastle three falls together on these conditions, {{...}}}}

===Etymology 2===
{{ety|en|id=packed|:der|frm:compact}}
From {{der|en|frm|compact}}, from {{der|en|la|compāctus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|compingō||join together}}, from {{m|la|com-||together}} + {{m|la|pangō||fasten}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*peh₂ǵ-||to attach, fix, fasten}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|more,er}}

# Closely packed or densely constituted; having much material in a small volume.
#: {{syn|en|concentrated|crowded|dense|serried|Thesaurus:compact}}
#: {{hypo|en|ultracompact}}
#* {{RQ:Newton Opticks|passage=glass, crystal, gems, and other '''compact''' bodies}}
# Having all necessary features fitting neatly into a small space.
#: {{ux|en|a '''compact''' laptop computer}}
# {{lb|en|of|_|prose}} [[brief|Brief]] and [[pithy]]; not [[verbose]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''compact''' discourse}}
# {{lb|en|topology|uncomparable}} {{non-gloss|Of a topological space:}}
## {{senseid|en|notnecessarilyhausdorff}} Such that every [[open cover]] has a [[finite]] [[subcover]]. In a [[metric space]], this is [[equivalent]] to being [[sequentially compact]]. In metric spaces with the {{w|Heine-Borel property}}, this is equivalent to being [[closed]] and [[bounded]].
##: {{syn|en|quasi-compact}}
## {{senseid|en|necessarilyhausdorff}} Compact in the above sense and moreover [[Hausdorff]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Joined or held together; leagued; confederated.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|act=V|scene=i|page=81|passage=Thou fooliſh Frier, and thou pernicious woman / '''Compact''' with her that's gone:}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1622|author={{w|Henry Peacham (born 1578)|Henry Peacham (Junior)}}|title=The Compleat Gentleman
|passage=a pipe of seven reeds, '''compact''' with wax together}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Composed or made; with ''of''.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=8|passage=A wandering fire, / '''Compact''' of unctuous vapor.|year=1873}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The two mathematical senses are favored by different mathematicians and in different branches of mathematics. Mathematicians who use '''compact''' in {{senseno|en|necessarilyhausdorff}} use the term [[quasi-compact]] for {{senseno|en|notnecessarilyhausdorff}}; this usage is particularly common in [[algebraic geometry]].

